# III. FUGUE  b46-73  (C minor, 4/4, Allegro moderato)
# Subject (cellos, b46-47): it begins with the sarabande's head
# (c g a-flat e-flat) and then slides down the chromatic lament
# c b b-flat a a-flat g.  Countersubject (b48-49): a long note, then
# four rising eighths - the line that refuses to sink.
#   b46-53  exposition, strings only, four voices (vc, va, vn2, vn1)
#   b54-56  episode 1: the strings place the cadence and fall silent;
#           oboe and clarinet toss the head down a chain of thirds
#           (C - A-flat - F - D-flat - B-flat7)
#   b57-58  middle entry in E-flat MAJOR, horn 1, dolce, with the
#           countersubject in the flute - the subject imagining
#           consolation
#   b59-63  episode 2: the head climbs by step (Cm/G-Ab, Dm/A-Bb,
#           Em/B-C), cellos and basses climb chromatically under it,
#           and a C pedal with the violins' d-flat 6 winds the spring
#   b64-65  bass entry in F minor (bassoons, cellos, basses), forte
#   b66-69  stretto by augmentation: trombones (first time forte) carry
#           the subject at half speed in C minor while the first
#           violins sing it at full speed above
#   b70-73  climax: the deceptive cadence (G -> A-flat) the sarabande
#           only hinted at, Neapolitan, Italian sixth, dominant - the
#           top line turns c db c b around the sarabande's own c-db
#           neighbour, the second violins climb eb f f# g against it -
#           and a cut-off into silence.

== b46-53 [G] ==
